## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about worst-case guarantees and practical trade-offs between quicksort and mergesort, including depth control, partition balance, and memory usage in a real-world data processing pipeline.

## Key Concepts
- Hybrid sorting (introspective sort)
- Worst-case guarantees, space complexity
- Handling duplicates, stability considerations
- Practical performance on sorted/partially sorted data

## Code Example
```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>

// Simplified hybrid sort: quicksort with depth limit, switch to mergesort when needed
int partition(std::vector<long long>& a, int l, int r) {
  long long pivot = a[(l + r) / 2];
  int i = l, j = r;
  while (i <= j) {
    while (a[i] < pivot) ++i;
    while (a[j] > pivot) --j;
    if (i <= j) std::swap(a[i++], a[j--]);
  }
  return i;
}

void mergeSort(std::vector<long long>& a, int l, int r, std::vector<long long>& tmp) {
  if (r - l <= 1) return;
  int m = (l + r) / 2;
  mergeSort(a, l, m, tmp);
  mergeSort(a, m, r, tmp);
  int i = l, j = m, k = 0;
  while (i < m || j < r) {
    if (j >= r || (i < m && a[i] <= a[j])) tmp[k++] = a[i++];
    else tmp[k++] = a[j++];
  }
  for (int t = 0; t < k; ++t) a[l + t] = tmp[t];
}

void hybridSort(std::vector<long long>& a, int l, int r, int depth, int depthLimit) {
  if (r - l <= 16) {
    std::sort(a.begin() + l, a.begin() + r + 1);
    return;
  }
  if (depth >= depthLimit) {
    std::vector<long long> tmp(r - l + 1);
    mergeSort(a, l, r + 1, tmp);
    return;
  }
  int p = partition(a, l, r);
  hybridSort(a, l, p - 1, depth + 1, depthLimit);
  hybridSort(a, p, r, depth + 1, depthLimit);
}

// Entry: call with depth=0 and depthLimit=2*log2(n)
```

## Why This Is Asked
Intersects BFS path counting and graph pruning under constraints; tests meticulous edge-case handling.

## Key Concepts
- BFS for unweighted shortest paths
- Path counting with modulo arithmetic
- Handling blocked nodes and unreachable cases
- Complexity: O(N+M)

## Code Example
```javascript
function shortestPaths(n, edges, blocked, s, t) {
  const g = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
  for (const [v, u] of edges) if (!blocked[v] && !blocked[u]) g[v].push(u);
  const dist = Array(n).fill(Infinity);
  const ways = Array(n).fill(0);
  if (blocked[s] || blocked[t]) return { dist: -1, ways: 0 };
  dist[s] = 0; ways[s] = 1;
  const q = [s];
  while (q.length) {
    const v = q.shift();
    for (const u of g[v]) {
      if (dist[u] === Infinity) {
        dist[u] = dist[v] + 1; ways[u] = ways[v]; q.push(u);
      } else if (dist[u] === dist[v] + 1) {
        ways[u] = (ways[u] + ways[v]) % 1000000007;
      }
    }
  }
  return { dist: dist[t] === Infinity ? -1 : dist[t], ways: ways[t] };
}
```

